   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public housing law, in relation to ensuring formerly\nincarcerated individuals have access to public housing",
    "basePrintNo": "S6895",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6895-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 18,
       "fullName": "Julia Salazar",
       "imgName": "1047_Julia_Salazar.jpg",
       "incumbent": true,
       "memberId": 1227,
       "sessionMemberId": 1833,
       "sessionYear": 2021,
       "shortName": "SALAZAR"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §402, Pub Hous L",
    "lawSection": "Public Housing Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6895\n\nSPONSOR: RIVERA\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public housing law, in relation to ensuring formerly\nincarcerated individuals have access to public housing\n\n \nPURPOSE OF BILL:\n\nTo ensure that previously incarcerated individuals have access to public\nhousing and that they do not face discrimination on the sole grounds of\nbeing a formerly incarcerated individual.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill amends section 402 of the public housing law by\nadding 2 a new subdivision 11 to read as follows: Notwithstanding any\nother provision of law to the contrary, no person shall be denied occu-\npancy in a dwelling in any project or be subjected to eviction from any\nsuch dwelling on the sole ground that such person is a formerly incar-\ncerated individual.\n\nSection 2 provides for the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIncarcerated individuals generally have a difficult time adjusting to\nlife after incarceration. The stigma of being a formerly incarcerated\nindividual, along with the discrimination endured when applying for\npublic housing, applying for employment, and socially, contributes to\nthe difficulty of assimilating back into society. As the population of\njailed New Yorkers continues to decline, more and more previously incar-\ncerated individuals are in need of affordable and accessible housing\noptions and are turning to public housing for relief.  Unfortunately,\nthe policies enforced by the New York City Housing Authority (NYCHA)\ndeny the right to public housing for previously incarcerated individ-\nuals. These practices tear families apart, making it so children are\nunable to live with their previously incarcerated family members, and\nproviding yet another hardship for these individuals as they reenter\nsociety.\n\nMore than half of the individuals who are released from state correc-\ntional facilities each year are released into homelessness, and discri-\nminatory public housing practices undoubtedly contribute to this issue.\nFormerly incarcerated individuals should have the same access to public\nhousing as any other New Yorker, and it is essential that these prejudi-\ncial policies be addressed as soon as possible to ensure that no more\nformerly incarcerated individuals are victimized by NYCHA's discrimina-\ntory practices. This bill prohibits formerly incarcerated individuals\nfrom being denied access to or eviction from public housing, solely on\nthe basis of being a formerly incarcerated individual. This bill would\nensure that access to New York State public housing becomes a feasible\noption for previously incarcerated individuals, and ensures that there\nis one less hurdle for these individuals to endure as they re-enter\nsociety.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:.\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
